WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.995
Welcome to Module 5 of the
HR-Driven Identity learning path:

2
00:00:03.995 --> 00:00:06.813
check certificate-based authentication.

3
00:00:06.813 --> 00:00:10.415
This is Gate 5 of the
implementation guide, credentials,

4
00:00:10.415 --> 00:00:13.862
and it goes with Chapter 05
of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:13.862 --> 00:00:16.325
A PIV card's revocation reaches sign-in

6
00:00:16.325 --> 00:00:19.326
only through the
certificate revocation list.

7
00:00:19.326 --> 00:00:23.559
So in this module you'll measure every
revocation list and check the tenant's

8
00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:27.995
certificate-based authentication settings
against the limits for your cloud:

9
00:00:27.995 --> 00:00:32.229
the trust store, each list against the
cloud's limit, the multifactor mapping,

10
00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:35.925
revocation list validation and
the phishing-resistant strength.

11
00:00:35.925 --> 00:00:39.281
You'll run the check on a
fictional tenant for GCC

12
00:00:39.281 --> 00:00:42.638
and then for GCC High, and
see the findings change.

13
00:00:42.638 --> 00:00:46.160
The module takes about
forty-five minutes with its lab.

14
00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:51.497
Why does this gate need code?

15
00:00:51.497 --> 00:00:55.410
Because revocation is where
card sign-in quietly breaks.

16
00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:59.749
If an issuing certificate authority in
the trust store has no revocation list

17
00:00:59.749 --> 00:01:04.422
configured, Entra doesn't check revocation
for that authority's certificates at all.

18
00:01:04.422 --> 00:01:06.335
A revoked card keeps working.

19
00:01:06.335 --> 00:01:09.411
If a list is configured but
can't be downloaded in time,

20
00:01:09.411 --> 00:01:13.885
certificate-based authentication fails
for every card from that authority.

21
00:01:13.885 --> 00:01:15.260
Nobody can sign in.

22
00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.505
And a revocation list grows
with every revocation,

23
00:01:18.505 --> 00:01:22.523
so a list that fits today can cross
your cloud's limit next month.

24
00:01:22.523 --> 00:01:25.923
None of those shows up in a
one-time configuration review.

25
00:01:25.923 --> 00:01:29.341
So this module measures each
list and checks the tenant's

26
00:01:29.341 --> 00:01:33.550
settings against your cloud's
limits, every day rather than once.

27
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.213
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of the module.

28
00:01:40.213 --> 00:01:43.876
You'll state the interactive
revocation list limits

29
00:01:43.876 --> 00:01:47.124
for GCC and GCC High, and
the download time limit.

30
00:01:47.124 --> 00:01:50.900
You'll measure a set of lists and
record the failures as findings.

31
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.728
You'll run the
certificate-based authentication

32
00:01:53.728 --> 00:01:56.488
check for a named cloud
and explain what it finds.

33
00:01:56.488 --> 00:01:59.013
And you'll explain why
the same list can be

34
00:01:59.013 --> 00:02:01.463
a warning in one cloud and not in another.

35
00:02:01.463 --> 00:02:05.843
Before you start, finish Module
3: there, the Temporary Access

36
00:02:05.843 --> 00:02:10.487
Pass bridges a new hire to the card,
and this gate ends that bridge.

37
00:02:10.487 --> 00:02:15.045
Some familiarity with P K I terms
helps, like issuing authority,

38
00:02:15.045 --> 00:02:20.304
revocation list and policy O I D;
Chapter 06 of the Federal Modernization

39
00:02:20.304 --> 00:02:24.774
Workbook's PKI and Certificates
book explains them for Entra.

40
00:02:24.774 --> 00:02:28.360
Lab 1 needs Python and
pytest, and no tenant.

41
00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:35.897
Unit 1 starts in the guide's Step
5, bind credentials to HR events.

42
00:02:35.897 --> 00:02:40.224
Its first step gives the revocation
list limits for each cloud,

43
00:02:40.224 --> 00:02:45.798
and its later steps tie card issuance and
revocation to HR and contractor events.

44
00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:51.360
Chapter 09 of the HR-Driven Identity book
explains why revocation checking matters,

45
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.299
and that a reissued card
changes the binding

46
00:02:54.299 --> 00:02:57.398
between the PIV certificate
and the Entra user.

47
00:02:57.398 --> 00:02:59.597
Notice what this module doesn't cover.

48
00:02:59.597 --> 00:03:05.204
Binding coverage and affinity for each
user belong to the validation kit's check,

49
00:03:05.204 --> 00:03:09.723
V K PIV oh one, and to Gate 6 of the
attribute-driven migration guide.

50
00:03:09.723 --> 00:03:12.095
This module checks the tenant's settings:

51
00:03:12.095 --> 00:03:14.886
the certificate authorities
in the trust store,

52
00:03:14.886 --> 00:03:19.282
the X dot five-oh-nine certificate
method, the authentication strengths and

53
00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:24.100
the Conditional Access policies, plus
a measurement of each revocation list.

54
00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:29.800
Here are the limits the
check holds each cloud to.

55
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.015
GCC uses the commercial service,

56
00:03:32.015 --> 00:03:37.100
so its interactive sign-in limit for
a revocation list is twenty megabytes.

57
00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:41.249
GCC High is Azure Government,
at forty-five megabytes,

58
00:03:41.249 --> 00:03:43.700
and the kit holds DoD to the same.

59
00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.275
In every cloud a list must
download within ten seconds.

60
00:03:47.275 --> 00:03:52.196
Learn gives those limits in megabytes;
the kit reads them as decimal megabytes,

61
00:03:52.196 --> 00:03:55.375
a million bytes each, which
is the stricter reading.

62
00:03:55.375 --> 00:03:59.522
There's no default cloud in the
kit: you name it every time,

63
00:03:59.522 --> 00:04:03.850
so a GCC High check can't silently
run against GCC's numbers.

64
00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:07.370
Two thresholds are the program's
design guidance, not Learn's:

65
00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:10.468
a warning at eighty percent
of the interactive limit,

66
00:04:10.468 --> 00:04:15.120
and a warning when an authority's
certificate expires within ninety days.

67
00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.457
Unit 2 has two parts, the
measurement and the check.

68
00:04:22.457 --> 00:04:27.619
The measurement downloads each revocation
list once and records its size in bytes,

69
00:04:27.619 --> 00:04:31.433
the seconds it took and any error,
with the time it was measured.

70
00:04:31.433 --> 00:04:35.115
An unreachable list isn't an
exception that stops the run:

71
00:04:35.115 --> 00:04:39.345
it's recorded, because the failure
is exactly the finding you want.

72
00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:42.240
For the lab, a local
path is read as a file,

73
00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.858
which is how Lab 1 runs on
a laptop with no network.

74
00:04:45.858 --> 00:04:50.095
In Lab 2 you measure the real lists
from outside the agency network.

75
00:04:50.095 --> 00:04:52.258
And the check insists on the measurement.

76
00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:55.574
A configured list with no
measurement row is C B six,

77
00:04:55.574 --> 00:04:59.870
a Finding, so you can't pass the
check by skipping the download.

78
00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.670
The check runs thirteen rules,
C B one to C B thirteen.

79
00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:10.358
Eight of them look at the
trust store and the lists.

80
00:05:10.358 --> 00:05:15.820
C B twelve: an issuing authority the card
program uses isn't in the trust store.

81
00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:19.722
C B one: an authority has no
revocation list configured,

82
00:05:19.722 --> 00:05:22.583
so Entra doesn't check revocation for it.

83
00:05:22.583 --> 00:05:25.559
A root with no list is
only a warning to confirm,

84
00:05:25.559 --> 00:05:30.182
since a root that issues only authority
certificates may publish none.

85
00:05:30.182 --> 00:05:36.196
C B two: the list's endpoint isn't
plain H T T P, which Learn recommends.

86
00:05:36.196 --> 00:05:38.470
C B six: no measurement.

87
00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:43.408
C B four: the list couldn't be downloaded,
or took longer than ten seconds.

88
00:05:43.408 --> 00:05:47.432
C B three: the list is over
the cloud's interactive limit.

89
00:05:47.432 --> 00:05:50.032
C B five warns at eighty percent of it.

90
00:05:50.032 --> 00:05:55.370
And C B eleven warns when an authority's
certificate expires within ninety days.

91
00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.207
The other five rules look at
the method and the policy.

92
00:06:02.207 --> 00:06:07.146
C B seven: the X dot five-oh-nine
certificate method isn't enabled.

93
00:06:07.146 --> 00:06:12.675
C B eight warns when revocation list
validation isn't required of every authority,

94
00:06:12.675 --> 00:06:15.358
or when an authority is exempted from it.

95
00:06:15.358 --> 00:06:18.418
An exemption is allowed
only while that authority's

96
00:06:18.418 --> 00:06:20.696
list is being fixed, and with an end date.

97
00:06:20.696 --> 00:06:25.951
C B nine: a PIV policy O I D isn't
mapped to multifactor, and the default

98
00:06:25.951 --> 00:06:31.207
mode is single-factor, so a card
sign-in may not count as multifactor.

99
00:06:31.207 --> 00:06:35.283
C B thirteen warns about
low-affinity username bindings.

100
00:06:35.283 --> 00:06:39.753
And C B ten is the SCuBA rule: no
enabled Conditional Access policy

101
00:06:39.753 --> 00:06:44.720
for all users and all resources
requires a phishing-resistant strength.

102
00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:48.964
A strength counts only if every
combination it allows is FIDO2,

103
00:06:48.964 --> 00:06:53.010
Windows Hello for Business,
or multifactor certificate.

104
00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:57.485
Try it.

105
00:06:57.485 --> 00:07:01.217
Lab 1 runs in the h r
lifecycle examples folder,

106
00:07:01.217 --> 00:07:04.360
against a fictional P K I, with no tenant.

107
00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.672
Step one lists what the reader will call.

108
00:07:06.672 --> 00:07:10.267
The task asks for three permissions,
Policy dot Read dot All,

109
00:07:10.267 --> 00:07:14.814
Policy dot Read dot Authentication
Method, and Public Key Infrastructure

110
00:07:14.814 --> 00:07:18.335
dot Read dot All, for five calls,
and every one of them is a GET.

111
00:07:18.335 --> 00:07:20.172
Nothing here can change the tenant.

112
00:07:20.172 --> 00:07:23.322
Step two measures the
fixture revocation lists.

113
00:07:23.322 --> 00:07:26.236
Two files measure at four
and eight kilobytes.

114
00:07:26.236 --> 00:07:29.523
The third doesn't exist, and
the tool records it as zero

115
00:07:29.523 --> 00:07:32.885
bytes with a file-not-found
error, rather than stopping.

116
00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:35.535
That row is what becomes
a finding in the check.

117
00:07:35.535 --> 00:07:37.861
Your times may differ from the slide;

118
00:07:37.861 --> 00:07:42.010
the fixtures are local files,
so they read almost instantly.

119
00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:49.510
Step three checks the
fictional tenant for GCC.

120
00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:54.672
The first line names the limits it's using:
twenty million bytes, and ten seconds.

121
00:07:54.672 --> 00:07:59.210
Then the count: five Findings,
seven Warns, and exit code two.

122
00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.260
The lab asks you to find four of them.

123
00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:06.272
C B four: one authority's list took
eleven point four seconds to download,

124
00:08:06.272 --> 00:08:11.285
over the ten-second limit, so in real
life sign-in would fail for its cards.

125
00:08:11.285 --> 00:08:15.485
C B five: that same list is
eighteen and a half million bytes,

126
00:08:15.485 --> 00:08:18.460
more than eighty percent of the GCC limit.

127
00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:23.648
C B nine: the PIV policy O I
D isn't mapped to multifactor.

128
00:08:23.648 --> 00:08:28.623
And C B ten: no enabled policy requires
a phishing-resistant strength.

129
00:08:28.623 --> 00:08:32.060
You'll also see a legacy
intermediate with no list at all,

130
00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:35.790
and an issuing authority
missing from the trust store.

131
00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:40.953
What differs by cloud?

132
00:08:40.953 --> 00:08:46.352
Step four runs the same check on the
same tenant, naming GCC High instead.

133
00:08:46.352 --> 00:08:49.630
The limit line now says
forty-five million bytes,

134
00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.990
and the count drops to five
Findings and six Warns.

135
00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:55.665
One warning went away: C B five.

136
00:08:55.665 --> 00:09:00.965
The eighteen and a half million byte list
was over eighty percent of GCC's twenty

137
00:09:00.965 --> 00:09:04.820
megabytes, but it's well under
eighty percent of forty-five,

138
00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:06.265
so in GCC High it's fine.

139
00:09:06.265 --> 00:09:08.027
The download time is different.

140
00:09:08.027 --> 00:09:10.792
The ten-second limit
applies in every cloud,

141
00:09:10.792 --> 00:09:14.553
so the eleven point four second
download is still a Finding.

142
00:09:14.553 --> 00:09:18.745
That's the lesson of this step: the
same list can be a warning in one cloud

143
00:09:18.745 --> 00:09:23.300
and not in another, which is why the
check always takes the cloud by name.

144
00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:30.097
Lab 2 moves to your own test
tenant, never production,

145
00:09:30.097 --> 00:09:35.200
with certificate-based authentication
configured for a test certificate authority.

146
00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.722
You create a reader app with a
certificate that holds only the

147
00:09:38.722 --> 00:09:42.175
three permissions you listed in
step one, with admin consent.

148
00:09:42.175 --> 00:09:47.608
You export the configuration, measure the
real revocation lists from outside the

149
00:09:47.608 --> 00:09:50.405
agency network, and
run the check with your

150
00:09:50.405 --> 00:09:52.962
test P K I's thumbprints
and policy O I Ds.

151
00:09:52.962 --> 00:09:55.530
Then the proof: revoke a test certificate,

152
00:09:55.530 --> 00:10:00.137
and confirm Entra refuses it after
the next revocation list is published.

153
00:10:00.137 --> 00:10:04.948
To back it out, remove the permissions,
delete the reader app and its certificate,

154
00:10:04.948 --> 00:10:07.935
reissue the test user's
certificate if you need it,

155
00:10:07.935 --> 00:10:10.413
and delete the export and output folders.

156
00:10:10.413 --> 00:10:12.920
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

157
00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:18.057
The federal requirement.

158
00:10:18.057 --> 00:10:23.945
This gate meets two controls in NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three:

159
00:10:23.945 --> 00:10:27.982
I A two, enhancement twelve,
acceptance of PIV credentials,

160
00:10:27.982 --> 00:10:32.271
and I A five, enhancement two,
public key-based authentication.

161
00:10:32.271 --> 00:10:36.716
It meets SCuBA's two phishing-resistant
multifactor policies,

162
00:10:36.716 --> 00:10:41.845
three point one and three point
six, which BOD 25-01 requires.

163
00:10:41.845 --> 00:10:46.599
And it meets FIPS two oh one dash
three's issuance and termination rules,

164
00:10:46.599 --> 00:10:50.708
and special publication eight
hundred sixty-three dash four.

165
00:10:50.708 --> 00:10:55.642
The evidence an ISSO keeps for Gate
5: the configuration export and

166
00:10:55.642 --> 00:11:00.757
the check's findings and manifest,
dated, with each list's measurement.

167
00:11:00.757 --> 00:11:05.258
The C B nine and C B ten rows,
beside the ScubaGear report.

168
00:11:05.258 --> 00:11:10.646
Lab 2's refused sign-in, with the revocation
record and the list that published it.

169
00:11:10.646 --> 00:11:13.030
And the mode rules in the export.

170
00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:20.280
Check yourself with two questions
from the module's knowledge check.

171
00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:23.850
First: what happens when an
issuing certificate authority

172
00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:27.055
in the trust store has no
revocation list configured?

173
00:11:27.055 --> 00:11:28.018
The answer is b.

174
00:11:28.018 --> 00:11:30.667
Entra doesn't check revocation for that

175
00:11:30.667 --> 00:11:33.717
authority's certificates,
which is rule C B one.

176
00:11:33.717 --> 00:11:37.624
Second: an eighteen and
a half million byte list

177
00:11:37.624 --> 00:11:41.030
raises C B five for GCC
but not for GCC High.

178
00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:41.718
Why?

179
00:11:41.718 --> 00:11:42.727
The answer is b.

180
00:11:42.727 --> 00:11:47.183
C B five warns at eighty percent
of the cloud's interactive limit,

181
00:11:47.183 --> 00:11:52.480
and GCC High's limit is forty-five megabytes,
so the same size is under the line.

182
00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:57.710
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that teaches it.

183
00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:02.285
To sum up.

184
00:12:02.285 --> 00:12:07.584
You learned the interactive revocation
list limits, twenty megabytes for

185
00:12:07.584 --> 00:12:12.797
GCC and forty-five for GCC High, with
ten seconds to download in every cloud.

186
00:12:12.797 --> 00:12:14.354
You measured three lists,

187
00:12:14.354 --> 00:12:18.284
and the missing one became a
recorded finding instead of a crash.

188
00:12:18.284 --> 00:12:21.975
You checked a fictional tenant
for GCC and then for GCC High,

189
00:12:21.975 --> 00:12:27.097
and saw how the cloud's limit changes the
findings while the download limit doesn't.

190
00:12:27.097 --> 00:12:30.193
Keep the measurement and
the check running every day,

191
00:12:30.193 --> 00:12:33.710
because a list that fits today
can cross the limit next month.

192
00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:37.056
Next is Module 6, time
separations from the logs:

193
00:12:37.056 --> 00:12:41.860
separation-to-block and
separation-to-revocation for every leaver.

194
00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:44.897
It builds on this module,
because a card's revocation

195
00:12:44.897 --> 00:12:47.560
reaches sign-in through
the revocation list.
